  "question": {
    "text": "A spherical ball of mass&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>m</mi></math> is kept at the highest point in the space between two fixed, concentric spheres&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> and&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi></math> (see fig.). The smaller sphere&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi></math> has a radius&nbsp;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>R</mi></math> and space between the two spheres has a width <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>d</mi></math>. The ball has a diameter very slightly less than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>d</mi></math>. All surfaces are frictionless. The ball is given a gentle push (towards the right in the figure). The angle made by the radius vector of the ball with the upward vertical is denoted by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>θ</mi></math>.&nbsp; What is the total normal reaction force exerted by the spheres on the ball in terms of angle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>θ</mi></math>?<br />\r<br />\r<img alt=\"\" height=\"124\" src=\"https://cdn-question-pool.getmarks.app/nta_abhyas/jee_main/images/EM0007554_Ques1_1.jpg\" style=\"max-width:100%; object-fit:contain\" width=\"166\" />",
    "image": null
  },
